  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday...\n\nA cold front will continue moving across the region this evening.\nAhead of the cold front, conditions will be favorable for \ncritical fire weather conditions. Relative humidities will be in \nthe teens for Southeast Montana and North Central Wyoming with \nwinds gusting into the 30s mph. The cold frontal passage will \nincrease relative humidity values quickly, ending critical fire \nweather conditions. Red Flag Warnings have been issued for these \nlocations through midnight tonight. Strong winds behind the cold \nfront will bring winds gusting into the 30s and 40s mph overnight \nwith the strongest winds expected for Wheatland County and areas \nnear the Dakotas border.\n\nThis evening will see much of the precipitation over the mountains\nand foothills change to snow behind the cold front. Snow amounts\nof 2 inches or less are expected for the western foothills. Snow\nand strong winds will combine to create hazardous driving\nconditions for Judith Gap. Winds will decrease overnight leading \nto improved conditions by morning. A Winter Weather Advisory has \nbeen issued for the Judith Gap area through 6 AM Saturday. The \nrest of the area will receive rain with the potential for a brief \nrain/snow mix tonight with little to no snow accumulation. \nPrecipitation chances will come to an end by around 3 AM Saturday \nmorning. The greatest precipitation amounts are expected north and\nwest of Billings where there is a 30-70% chance of getting \ngreater than 0.5 inches of precipitation. Locations east of \nRosebud County are expected to receive little to no precipitation.\n\nWeather conditions will be very different Saturday with \ntemperatures in the 40s F and light winds. The exception to this \nis locations along the Dakotas border where winds will continue to\ngust into the 20s mph through the afternoon.\n\nMonday will see a 500 mb trough impact the Northern Plains.\nEnsemble systems currently indicate that this system will\nprimarily impact the Dakotas but will bring low precipitation\nchances (20-40%) to Southeast Montana. Precipitation amounts are\nexpected to remain light with the chance for greater than 0.1\ninches in the 10-30% range. Tuesday and Wednesday, upper level \ntroughing is expected to move in from the West Coast bringing \nadditional precipitation chances (40-60%). The chance for greater \nthan 0.25 inches of precipitation Tuesday/Wednesday ranges from \n20% near the Dakotas border to 50% for foothills locations. \nTemperatures are expected to be steady next week in the 40s and \n50s F. Torgerson",
